\details{
\subsection{How each spell relates to the earlier ones}{

Within a unit, spells are taken in order of start, and each is compared
with the latest end among the unit's earlier spells:
\itemize{
\item \code{gap}: it starts after a gap. The gap lengths are summarised.
\item \code{adjacent}: it starts the day (or period) after an earlier spell ends.
Two adjacent spells are often one spell recorded twice, e.g. a
transfer between wards or employers, and may need merging with
\code{\link[=merge_spells]{merge_spells()}} before spells are counted.
\item \code{touching}: it starts on the day an earlier spell ends. Spells are
closed intervals, so the two share that day and overlap by one day,
the usual way a discharge and a readmission on the same day look.
\item \code{overlapping}: it starts inside an earlier spell and ends after it.
\item \code{nested}: it lies entirely within an earlier spell, or starts after
an ongoing one.
\item \code{duplicate}: it has the same start and end as the spell before it.
}

Spells with the same start are taken longest first, so a shorter one is
\code{nested} in the longer. A missing end means an ongoing spell. Spells
with a missing start, or an end before the start, are counted and left
out of the rest.

\code{start} and \code{end} may be dates, compared by day, or whole numbers such as
years or month numbers, compared by that unit.
}
